the key difference between the crown  jewels and the Queen's Private  Collection is that the private  collection we know much less about these  are gifts that were given to the queen  over the Decades of her Reign but the  crown jewels belong to the Monarch they  are handed from Monarch to Monarch in  trust for the nation so technically they  belong to the Monarch but the Monarch  isn't allowed to run off with the crown  jewels they stay guarded at the Tower of  London in safe keeping for the nation  and when one Monarch dies the crown  jewels are immediately passed to their  Heir the queen has a very sizable and  very valuable Private Jewelry collection  and she has actually loaned in the past  quite a few pieces out to other female  members of the royal family for example  when Kate went to New Zealand the queen  loaned her a diamond Fern to wear  because that's the national symbol of  New Zealand when she went to Canada the  queen lent her the maple brooch which  had been gifted to the Queen by the  nation so these are quite emblematic  pieces of jewelry more often than not  they are diamonds they are worth a huge  amount of money and while we won't get  to see details of the Queen's private  will because it is exactly that private  I think over the coming months and years  we will see exactly whom she's  distributed her jewelry to because they  will be wearing it we've already seen  the queen pass over quite a few of her  pieces particularly to The Duchess of  Cambridge there is a hierarchy in all of  this the queen consort really gets first  choice of the Queen's jewelry and after  that it is the Princess of Wales of  course Kate The Duchess of Sussex I'm  sure will come in for some Jewelery at  some point but she is much further down  the picking order we saw Kate wearing a  pair of pearl earrings a present from  the queen for that um procession as they  went to the lying in state so jewelry is  used by the royal family as a very  subtle way of paying a message sending a  message whether that's wearing the  national emblem of a country when they  go to visit that country or in the case  of the Princess of Wales just paying  tribute to the Queen by wearing those  beautiful pearl earrings that the queen  had given to her Megan Duchess of Sussex  was seen wearing a pair of pearl  earrings that she'd been gifted from the  queen and she chose to wear them for  that service at the procession to the  lying of state so again the jewelry is  really important it might not  necessarily be worth a fortune but the  sentimental value of that you can't  really put a price on it there are some  very iconic pieces of jewelry and that  have been passed through the ages the  love is not Tiara for example which is  said to be worth 4 million based on the  intricate design of pearls and diamonds  is now been passed on to Princess of  Wales and the Princess of Wales before  Kate wore it but she used to complain it  was so heavy she didn't enjoy wearing it  at all in fact the queen had given it to  Lady Diana Spencer to wear on her  wedding day but it was so heavy for  Diana that she chose to wear the Spencer  Tiara instead Kate seems to manage  wearing it with gray great comfort and  that is Kate's go-to Tiara of choice so  more often than not if it's a state  reception or a banquet you'll see Kate  wearing that Lover's not Tiara which as  I say is worth 4 million but again when  you consider its history and its  Heritage and the Queens who have worn it  upon their heads in the past  it's in value you know it's hard to put  a value on it  [Music]
 

